able', 'results': [], 'summary': {}}, 'iab_categories_result': {'status': 'success', 'results': [{'text': "You. Hello and welcome. It's January 26, 2023. We're here in active inference model stream number 8.1. Today we're appreciative to have Thomas Ringstrom, who will be presenting on Reward is Not Necessary, a compositional theory of Self Preserving Agents with Empowerment gain maximization. There will be a presentation followed by a discussion. So Thomas, thank you for joining. Really looking forward to this. Off to you. Yeah, thank you very much.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.04588864743709564, 'label': 'PersonalFinance>PersonalInvesting'}, {'relevance': 0.006368451286107302, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Economy>Commodities'}, {'relevance': 0.004701113328337669, 'label': 'VideoGaming>VideoGameGenres>SimulationVideoGames'}, {'relevance': 0.00456009479239583, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>ArtificialIntelligence'}, {'relevance': 0.003798210294917226, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Economy>InterestRates'}, {'relevance': 0.003359506605193019, 'label': 'PersonalFinance>PersonalInvesting>Options'}, {'relevance': 0.003354649990797043, 'label': 'PersonalFinance>RetirementPlanning'}, {'relevance': 0.0024715824984014034, 'label': 'EventsAndAttractions>PoliticalEvent'}, {'relevance': 0.00171750714071095, 'label': 'Sports>FantasySports'}, {'relevance': 0.001617221045307815, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Economy>JobMarket'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 1170, 'end': 39186}, 'sentences_idx_start': 0, 'sentences_idx_end': 9}, {'text': "It's really nice to be here and talk to this group. I'm a computer science PhD student at the University of Minnesota. My interests are in what are the computational properties that we would need to have agents which flexibly plan in sort of high dimensional product spaces of variables, and also how do we get agents to perform complex tasks in an intrinsically motivated way, especially in high dimensional product spaces.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.9865731000900269, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>ArtificialIntelligence'}, {'relevance': 0.3037742078304291, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing'}, {'relevance': 0.06404269486665726, 'label': 'VideoGaming>VideoGameGenres>SimulationVideoGames'}, {'relevance': 0.048498425632715225, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Robotics'}, {'relevance': 0.017768651247024536, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ProgrammingLanguages'}, {'relevance': 0.00973971001803875, 'label': 'Technology&Computing'}, {'relevance': 0.0031749666668474674, 'label': 'Hobbies&Interests>GamesAndPuzzles'}, {'relevance': 0.0028285470325499773, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Business>BusinessI.T.'}, {'relevance': 0.002645132364705205, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>Internet>InternetOfThings'}, {'relevance': 0.0021556068677455187, 'label': 'Automotive>AutoType>DriverlessCars'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 39288, 'end': 84270}, 'sentences_idx_start': 10, 'sentences_idx_end': 12}, {'text': "So this presentation is going to argue that reward I know I'm talking to a sort of active inference crowd, but some of the same points apply to active inference perhaps too. But this presentation is mostly about how there's going to be some major problems. I think using reward maximization objectives, and by moving to sort of reward free objective functions, we can get really nice factorizations that help us plan. So let's just start off with a sort of simple, picture simplified picture of an organism.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.3448854684829712, 'label': 'Science>BiologicalSciences'}, {'relevance': 0.011389787308871746, 'label': 'Science>Genetics'}, {'relevance': 0.0037626067642122507, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Industries>BiotechAndBiomedicalIndustry'}, {'relevance': 0.0036776105407625437, 'label': 'Pets>FishAndAquariums'}, {'relevance': 0.0010708582121878862, 'label': 'Hobbies&Interests>Beekeeping'}, {'relevance': 0.001013009692542255, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing'}, {'relevance': 0.0008723887731321156, 'label': 'Science>Environment'}, {'relevance': 0.000868079368956387, 'label': 'MedicalHealth>DiseasesAndConditions>InfectiousDiseases'}, {'relevance': 0.000864317815285176, 'label': 'Science'}, {'relevance': 0.0007397723966278136, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>ArtificialIntelligence'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 85650, 'end': 131002}, 'sentences_idx_start': 13, 'sentences_idx_end': 16}, {'text': "So we have a honey badger here. This honey badger has internal states. It gets hungry and it gets thirsty. And there's also an external world that the agent lives in. And in order to sort of modify internal state spaces, the agent might have to perform some complex tasks. It might have to get several items in order to eat an apple or things like that.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.5679419040679932, 'label': 'Hobbies&Interests>Beekeeping'}, {'relevance': 0.0017398843774572015, 'label': 'Pets>Birds'}, {'relevance': 0.0013961757067590952, 'label': 'Pets>LargeAnimals'}, {'relevance': 0.0008357904152944684, 'label': 'Science>BiologicalSciences'}, {'relevance': 0.0006629728595726192, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Robotics'}, {'relevance': 0.0004155375645495951, 'label': 'Pets>FishAndAquariums'}, {'relevance': 0.00039676789310760796, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>Internet>InternetOfThings'}, {'relevance': 0.0003924261836800724, 'label': 'NewsAndPolitics>Disasters'}, {'relevance': 0.00037479758611880243, 'label': 'MedicalHealth>DiseasesAndConditions>InfectiousDiseases'}, {'relevance': 0.00027925538597628474, 'label': 'MedicalHealth>DiseasesAndConditions>Allergies'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 131066, 'end': 167210}, 'sentences_idx_start': 17, 'sentences_idx_end': 22}, {'text': "So you could see that maybe some symbolic state space sort of mediates the connection between things you do in the world and transformations that you make on some internal state space. And of course, real world organisms and humans live in a high dimensional world. So you could imagine that there's an encoder and a decoder and really there's a sort of high dimensional physiological or interoceptive domain that is these Y tilde with the dot or Y tilde, I should say, and Z tilde.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.032654013484716415, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>ArtificialIntelligence'}, {'relevance': 0.015886424109339714, 'label': 'Hobbies&Interests>ParanormalPhenomena'}, {'relevance': 0.014356180094182491, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>VirtualReality'}, {'relevance': 0.01106804609298706, 'label': 'MedicalHealth>DiseasesAndConditions>EyeAndVisionConditions'}, {'relevance': 0.010956269688904285, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>AugmentedReality'}, {'relevance': 0.006852141581475735, 'label': 'Science>Physics'}, {'relevance': 0.005502942018210888, 'label': 'Religion&Spirituality>Spirituality'}, {'relevance': 0.005345460493117571, 'label': 'MedicalHealth>DiseasesAndConditions>BrainAndNervousSystemDisorders'}, {'relevance': 0.0046262298710644245, 'label': 'Science'}, {'relevance': 0.0020128616597503424, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>3-DGraphics'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 167280, 'end': 209186}, 'sentences_idx_start': 23, 'sentences_idx_end': 25}, {'text': "And then there's a high dimensional world. You could just say that's x tilde. And really you just need to sort of map these down to some discrete state space in which these domains can interact here's like a simple encoder and decoder. If an agent has a kind of simple ontology like this, you can imagine that it's sort of generatively entrained to the world that it lives in, which is a notion that's probably pretty familiar to sort of active inference people.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.29906678199768066, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>ArtificialIntelligence'}, {'relevance': 0.017003526911139488, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>Internet>InternetOfThings'}, {'relevance': 0.013525173999369144, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing'}, {'relevance': 0.005991093814373016, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Robotics'}, {'relevance': 0.0028355023823678493, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>DataStorageAndWarehousing'}, {'relevance': 0.0028262832202017307, 'label': 'VideoGaming>VideoGameGenres>SimulationVideoGames'}, {'relevance': 0.0027785601560026407, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ProgrammingLanguages'}, {'relevance': 0.00240514799952507, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>Databases'}, {'relevance': 0.001883217366412282, 'label': 'Science'}, {'relevance': 0.0018187774112448096, 'label': 'Home&Garden>SmartHome'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 209298, 'end': 251640}, 'sentences_idx_start': 26, 'sentences_idx_end': 29}, {'text': "So you have some encoder of these high dimensional states and then you have some operator PS, which is in the middle of the head here. And that just sort of advances the latent states. And then you decode and you'll get sort of expectations of the high dimensional world that the agent lives in. The problem is that you can't really represent explicitly these latent space transition operators that would dictate the dynamics of all these variables.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.03493541106581688, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>ArtificialIntelligence'}, {'relevance': 0.023159775882959366, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Robotics'}, {'relevance': 0.01019507646560669, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing'}, {'relevance': 0.009817156940698624, 'label': 'VideoGaming>VideoGameGenres>SimulationVideoGames'}, {'relevance': 0.009131726808845997, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ProgrammingLanguages'}, {'relevance': 0.0064307404682040215, 'label': 'Science>Physics'}, {'relevance': 0.003817171324044466, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>OperatingSystems'}, {'relevance': 0.003369503188878298, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>3-DGraphics'}, {'relevance': 0.0011854638578370214, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>VirtualReality'}, {'relevance': 0.0011614729883149266, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>Internet>InternetOfThings'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 253130, 'end': 289886}, 'sentences_idx_start': 30, 'sentences_idx_end': 33}, {'text': "You can't represent it as an explicit object because the more state spaces that you keep track of in the world, the larger this object PS becomes. So in order to handle this, you'd have to represent it in a sort of factorized form. So you just would represent its factors. You wouldn't explicitly sort of enumerate all of the state vector transitions under this transition operator.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.010652149096131325, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>Databases'}, {'relevance': 0.0056922403164207935, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>DataStorageAndWarehousing'}, {'relevance': 0.005022972822189331, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ProgrammingLanguages'}, {'relevance': 0.004575653467327356, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>OperatingSystems'}, {'relevance': 0.0005481938715092838, 'label': 'VideoGaming>VideoGameGenres>SimulationVideoGames'}, {'relevance': 0.000491021666675806, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>3-DGraphics'}, {'relevance': 0.0004325302434153855, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing'}, {'relevance': 0.0004010352131444961, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Robotics'}, {'relevance': 0.00039874567301012576, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerPeripherals'}, {'relevance': 0.00032551062759011984, 'label': 'VideoGaming>VideoGameGenres>StrategyVideoGames'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 289918, 'end': 319290}, 'sentences_idx_start': 34, 'sentences_idx_end': 37}, {'text': 'And so what we really need to think about is what are the sort of model based or the sort of bellman principles for decomposing hierarchical state spaces so that we can create the right representations that help us plan in a flexible way in a sort of time varying, time dependent world of lots of variables. So this just means what is a sort of objective function l calligraphic L applied to PS or its factorization that would give us some factors here ada and these omegas that could help us plan.', 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.17054438591003418, 'label': 'VideoGaming>VideoGameGenres>SimulationVideoGames'}, {'relevance': 0.01637049950659275, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>DataStorageAndWarehousing'}, {'relevance': 0.0067494153045117855, 'label': 'VideoGaming>VideoGameGenres>StrategyVideoGames'}, {'relevance': 0.006229708902537823, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>3-DGraphics'}, {'relevance': 0.005510397255420685, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Business>BusinessOperations'}, {'relevance': 0.003421783447265625, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>Databases'}, {'relevance': 0.0018795012729242444, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications'}, {'relevance': 0.0015134606510400772, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Business>Logistics'}, {'relevance': 0.0013933510053902864, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ProgrammingLanguages'}, {'relevance': 0.001177520607598126, 'label': 'PersonalFinance>FinancialPlanning'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 319870, 'end': 369420}, 'sentences_idx_start': 38, 'sentences_idx_end': 39}, {'text': "And also, given the fact that the state space, the effective product space that we're working in is so large and there's many possible state vectors that describe possible states of the world, how do you know what to do? I mean, if you're an RL theorist, you would have to say, well, there exists some kind of reward function that tells you this state of the world is worth this much reward and this state of the world is worth this much reward.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.009189985692501068, 'label': 'VideoGaming>VideoGameGenres>SimulationVideoGames'}, {'relevance': 0.0032329410314559937, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Economy>InterestRates'}, {'relevance': 0.0030703358352184296, 'label': 'PersonalFinance>PersonalInvesting'}, {'relevance': 0.00252344086766243, 'label': 'PersonalFinance>PersonalInvesting>Options'}, {'relevance': 0.0018376066582277417, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>ArtificialIntelligence'}, {'relevance': 0.0013042438076809049, 'label': 'PersonalFinance>PersonalInvesting>StocksAndBonds'}, {'relevance': 0.000806029886007309, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Economy>Commodities'}, {'relevance': 0.0006808164180256426, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>VirtualReality'}, {'relevance': 0.0005110571510158479, 'label': 'PersonalFinance>RetirementPlanning'}, {'relevance': 0.0005070765619166195, 'label': 'Science>Physics'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 370030, 'end': 401202}, 'sentences_idx_start': 40, 'sentences_idx_end': 41}, {'text': "But it's not really clear how you should define reward functions on huge product spaces. So I will address this question from sort of reward free perspective. And just to give you a hint of what it's going to be like, what I'm going to argue is that what we really need are agents that have a kind of structured core ontology that it needs to maintain.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.28544288873672485, 'label': 'Shopping>SalesAndPromotions'}, {'relevance': 0.11094384640455246, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Business>MarketingAndAdvertising'}, {'relevance': 0.05239703506231308, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Business>Sales'}, {'relevance': 0.04258457571268082, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Economy>Commodities'}, {'relevance': 0.016981065273284912, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Business>BusinessOperations'}, {'relevance': 0.0084617268294096, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>DataStorageAndWarehousing'}, {'relevance': 0.006467333994805813, 'label': 'Hobbies&Interests>ContentProduction'}, {'relevance': 0.006086383014917374, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Business>Startups'}, {'relevance': 0.00576981483027339, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Industries>RetailIndustry'}, {'relevance': 0.004523623269051313, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>Internet>Search'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 401266, 'end': 428474}, 'sentences_idx_start': 42, 'sentences_idx_end': 44}, {'text': "That is coupled state spaces that really depend on each other, in which the policies that you use maintain the sort of internal integrity or controllability of the agent. And that's where empowerment will eventually come in. So I'll talk about empowerment a little too. That's the controllability metric.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.008833070285618305, 'label': 'EventsAndAttractions>PoliticalEvent'}, {'relevance': 0.005517846904695034, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>InformationAndNetworkSecurity'}, {'relevance': 0.0036972013767808676, 'label': 'NewsAndPolitics>Politics'}, {'relevance': 0.0024451320059597492, 'label': 'NewsAndPolitics>Politics>WarAndConflicts'}, {'relevance': 0.0022127777338027954, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>ArtificialIntelligence'}, {'relevance': 0.0018564511556178331, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Robotics'}, {'relevance': 0.0017373129958286881, 'label': 'NewsAndPolitics>Politics>PoliticalIssues'}, {'relevance': 0.0011000214144587517, 'label': 'VideoGaming>VideoGameGenres>SimulationVideoGames'}, {'relevance': 0.0009835121454671025, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>Internet>InternetOfThings'}, {'relevance': 0.0009472015080973506, 'label': 'NewsAndPolitics>Law'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 428522, 'end': 451510}, 'sentences_idx_start': 45, 'sentences_idx_end': 48}, {'text': "So here I'm just saying what's a nice policy or what's an objective function f that takes in some intrinsic motivation function, which is this fancy V here, along with a nice factorization that allows you to plan in the world. Okay? So just to recap things that I already said, we're in a product space of state of variables. We have internal and external state spaces, hunger and hydration state spaces can be dynamic. As you do things in the world, you get hungrier.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.014570879749953747, 'label': 'HealthyLiving'}, {'relevance': 0.012203596532344818, 'label': 'HealthyLiving>WeightLoss'}, {'relevance': 0.006555855739861727, 'label': 'Food&Drink>FoodMovements'}, {'relevance': 0.0030379651580005884, 'label': 'HealthyLiving>Wellness'}, {'relevance': 0.0028781730215996504, 'label': 'PersonalFinance>FrugalLiving'}, {'relevance': 0.002552848309278488, 'label': 'HealthyLiving>FitnessAndExercise'}, {'relevance': 0.0024769820738583803, 'label': 'PersonalFinance'}, {'relevance': 0.0021924814209342003, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Economy>InterestRates'}, {'relevance': 0.0016521369107067585, 'label': 'Food&Drink>HealthyCookingAndEating'}, {'relevance': 0.0012263614917173982, 'label': 'PersonalFinance>FinancialPlanning'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 451930, 'end': 488030}, 'sentences_idx_start': 49, 'sentences_idx_end': 53}, {'text': "So how do we plan? How do we know what a good goal is? And what I'm going to argue is that we should compute a specific representation called a state time feasibility function, which is going to be an abstraction that's going to map us from initial state times to final state times. And this will have some really nice properties that allow us to sort of reason in this high dimensional state space. And I'm going to talk about this from just a dynamic programming point of view.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.058825667947530746, 'label': 'VideoGaming>VideoGameGenres>SimulationVideoGames'}, {'relevance': 0.03168001398444176, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ProgrammingLanguages'}, {'relevance': 0.007178815081715584, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing'}, {'relevance': 0.004498486407101154, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>OperatingSystems'}, {'relevance': 0.0030493654776364565, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>ArtificialIntelligence'}, {'relevance': 0.002885917667299509, 'label': 'PersonalFinance>FinancialPlanning'}, {'relevance': 0.0024800957180559635, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>DataStorageAndWarehousing'}, {'relevance': 0.002053371164947748, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Business>BusinessOperations'}, {'relevance': 0.0015141842886805534, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications'}, {'relevance': 0.0013824787456542253, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Robotics'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 489090, 'end': 520554}, 'sentences_idx_start': 54, 'sentences_idx_end': 58}, {'text': "There isn't going to be any learning. Okay? So let's just talk about transition operator composition, because I said, well, we have this large product space of state variables and it'd be nice if we just represented it as a factorization. So what does that mean? So imagine that you have PX, which is like a base state space to move around the world, and you have some internal state space or secondary state space PY. And these are linked by some function f.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.18160292506217957, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ProgrammingLanguages'}, {'relevance': 0.001258395379409194, 'label': 'Science>Physics'}, {'relevance': 0.0008710701367817819, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>Databases'}, {'relevance': 0.0007585579878650606, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing'}, {'relevance': 0.0004167959850747138, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>3-DGraphics'}, {'relevance': 0.0003321910335216671, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>DataStorageAndWarehousing'}, {'relevance': 0.0002569390635471791, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>DigitalAudio'}, {'relevance': 0.0001815303839975968, 'label': 'VideoGaming>VideoGameGenres>SimulationVideoGames'}, {'relevance': 0.0001784725027391687, 'label': 'Education>EducationalAssessment'}, {'relevance': 0.000149240528116934, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Economy>InterestRates'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 520672, 'end': 549762}, 'sentences_idx_start': 59, 'sentences_idx_end': 64}, {'text': "So here's your base state space. And here's the honey badger in the state space. And here's the secondary state space, which is just like your internal hunger space. And so F, what F is going to be is called an availability function. And an availability function is going to say, oh, this honey badger is in state XAT at time T. And given that it's there, what's the probability that this goal is available from this state time, state action time.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.9028946161270142, 'label': 'Hobbies&Interests>Beekeeping'}, {'relevance': 0.0028151897713541985, 'label': 'Pets>Birds'}, {'relevance': 0.0005896821385249496, 'label': 'Pets>LargeAnimals'}, {'relevance': 0.0004268889606464654, 'label': 'Education>HomeworkAndStudy'}, {'relevance': 0.0002904713910538703, 'label': 'Food&Drink>FoodMovements'}, {'relevance': 0.00026284909108653665, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>Internet>CloudComputing'}, {'relevance': 0.000258249812759459, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>InformationAndNetworkSecurity'}, {'relevance': 0.00023877584317233413, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Industries>FoodIndustry'}, {'relevance': 0.00020137958927080035, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>AntivirusSoftware'}, {'relevance': 0.0001928062702063471, 'label': 'EventsAndAttractions>PersonalCelebrations&LifeEvents>Birth'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 549816, 'end': 589010}, 'sentences_idx_start': 65, 'sentences_idx_end': 70}, {'text': "And so this goal is formally going to be an action on the higher level state space. So for instance, these green lines on the high level state space are going to map you to the most satiated state where you're not hungry anymore at all. And then these black lines on the internal state space are just going to decrement you one state if you're not at the tree, so you eat apples at the tree and that's going to map you to the top.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.029977714642882347, 'label': 'HealthyLiving'}, {'relevance': 0.02753007970750332, 'label': 'Food&Drink>FoodMovements'}, {'relevance': 0.011089669540524483, 'label': 'HealthyLiving>WeightLoss'}, {'relevance': 0.009101863019168377, 'label': 'HealthyLiving>Nutrition'}, {'relevance': 0.006628557574003935, 'label': 'Food&Drink>HealthyCookingAndEating'}, {'relevance': 0.0032465902622789145, 'label': 'PersonalFinance>FrugalLiving'}, {'relevance': 0.0018249594140797853, 'label': 'HealthyLiving>Wellness'}, {'relevance': 0.0007630912004970014, 'label': 'HealthyLiving>SeniorHealth'}, {'relevance': 0.000622271210886538, 'label': 'PersonalFinance>FinancialAssistance>GovernmentSupportAndWelfare'}, {'relevance': 0.0006018643034622073, 'label': 'BusinessAndFinance>Business>GreenSolutions'}], 'timestamp': {'start': 589160, 'end': 619306}, 'sentences_idx_start': 71, 'sentences_idx_end': 73}, {'text': "And all other states without a tree, you're going to get hungrier over time. Okay, so if we wanted to write a product space operator PS y prime, x prime, we can just represent this as a composition where lambda P is our composition operator, and it's just going to be defined as the product of your two state spaces with F linking them. And then we sum over G. So we're getting rid of the goal variable and so we can represent the product space this way.", 'labels': [{'relevance': 0.08114853501319885, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ProgrammingLanguages'}, {'relevance': 0.004266013391315937, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>Databases'}, {'relevance': 0.000714685651473701, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>ComputerSoftwareAndApplications>OperatingSystems'}, {'relevance': 0.00040920492028817534, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>DataStorageAndWarehousing'}, {'relevance': 0.0004032587166875601, 'label': 'Technology&Computing>Computing>Computer